{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#1518","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#1518","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#1518\",\"parent_revision\":\"12d1e5ce2b5324a02dee78892d8ef1a6affe1cddf2ea6014a14791cb547528e8\",\"root_revision\":\"12d1e5ce2b5324a02dee78892d8ef1a6affe1cddf2ea6014a14791cb547528e8\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: \\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: \\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nEvery task method in GitHubTaskProvider (read_task, claim_task, update_task_status, update_task_fields, append_task_section, get_ready_tasks) calls _fetch_plan_node(plan_id) solely to verify the plan issue exists and has the sam:plan label. This is one extra GitHub GraphQL API call per task operation, before the actual sub-issue fetch. The existence check is redundant \u2014 a missing plan would produce an empty sub-issue list or an error from the sub-issue fetch naturally. Additionally, list_plans makes N+1 API calls: one to list plans, then one per plan to count sub-issues.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n[VERIFIED] `_fetch_plan_node` is called before every task operation.\\n\\nVerified call sites in `github_task.py`:\\n- `read_task` line 411: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `claim_task` line 416: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `update_task_status` line 428: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `update_task_fields` line 435: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `append_task_section` line 446: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `get_ready_tasks` line 455: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `store_document` line 492: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)`\\n- `update_plan_fields` line 391: `self._fetch_plan_node(plan_id)` (plan operation \u2014 arguably appropriate here)\\n\\n[VERIFIED] `_fetch_plan_node` issues a GitHub GraphQL API call per invocation (`_fetch_issue_graphql` at line 248), then validates the `sam:plan` label.\\n\\n[VERIFIED] `_fetch_task_nodes` (which follows for task operations) issues its own GitHub API call (`get_task_issues` at line 260). So every task read/write makes 2 API calls where 1 would suffice.\\n\\n[VERIFIED] `list_plans` N+1 pattern confirmed at lines 372: iterates plan nodes from `_fetch_issues_graphql`, then calls `self._issue_backend.get_task_issues(repo, node[\\\"number\\\"])` for each plan node to count sub-issues (line 372). N plans = N+1 total API calls.\\n\\n[VERIFIED \u2014 nuance] The `_fetch_plan_node` guard does provide one thing `_fetch_task_nodes` does not: it validates the `sam:plan` label. Without it, an operation on a valid GitHub issue that lacks the `sam:plan` label would silently proceed (returning an empty sub-issue list and raising `TaskNotFoundError` rather than `PlanNotFoundError`). This is a different error, not no error. The fix must preserve the `PlanNotFoundError` semantics.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Issue Classification\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nType: defect (performance / incorrect error semantics under certain conditions)\\n\\nRoot cause (5 whys):\\n1. Every task method makes 2 API calls. Why?\\n2. `_fetch_plan_node` is called before `_fetch_task_nodes` to guard against invalid plan IDs.\\n3. Why is the guard needed separately? Because `_fetch_task_nodes` only requires a valid integer and returns an empty list for unknown issue numbers \u2014 it does not verify the `sam:plan` label.\\n4. Why doesn't `_fetch_task_nodes` validate the label? It was written as a narrow fetcher focused on sub-issues, not plan validation.\\n5. Why was no combined fetch-and-validate path designed? The two concerns (plan validation, task fetching) were implemented independently without a coalesced path.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Files\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:241-268` \u2014 `_fetch_plan_node` and `_fetch_task_nodes` \u2014 the two separate fetchers to consolidate\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:361-385` \u2014 `list_plans` N+1 at line 372\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/core/backends/github_task.py:409-458` \u2014 task methods with redundant guard calls (lines 411, 416, 428, 435, 446, 455)\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- Every task read/write currently costs 2 GitHub GraphQL API calls. For a 10-task plan check-in sequence (claim + update_status per task), that is 40 API calls instead of 20.\\n- `list_plans` on a workspace with 50 plans issues 51 API calls. At GitHub's secondary rate limit this can produce `HTTP 403` for busy workspaces.\\n- `read_plan` (line 343-358) calls `_fetch_plan_node` then `_fetch_task_nodes` \u2014 the plan body is fetched but its node data is not reused by `_fetch_task_nodes`, which fetches sub-issues separately. One of those two calls is wasted.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Dependencies\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNo dependency on #1516 or #1517. Can be implemented independently.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Task methods (`read_task`, `claim_task`, `update_task_status`, `update_task_fields`, `append_task_section`, `get_ready_tasks`) make exactly 1 GitHub API call for the sub-issue fetch, not 2.\\n- [ ] `PlanNotFoundError` is still raised when the issue does not exist or lacks the `sam:plan` label \u2014 the label validation is not silently dropped.\\n- [ ] A combined helper (e.g. `_fetch_plan_and_tasks(plan_id)`) fetches both the plan node and sub-issues in a single round-trip (or validates the label from the sub-issue parent data if the IssueBackend supports it).\\n- [ ] `list_plans` task_count is obtained without N additional `get_task_issues` calls \u2014 either via a sub-issue count field on the plan node returned by `_fetch_issues_graphql`, or deferred (return `task_count=0` or `None` with a note that it requires a separate call).\\n- [ ] `read_plan` reuses the plan node already fetched rather than fetching it twice (once for plan metadata, once implicitly through the task fetch path).\\n- [ ] Existing integration tests for `GitHubTaskProvider` pass.\\n- [ ] `uv run prek run --files <changed files>` exits 0.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Effort\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nEstimate: M\\nReasoning: Requires understanding the IssueBackend Protocol to know what combined queries are available. The combined-fetch helper needs to be designed carefully to preserve `PlanNotFoundError` semantics. The `list_plans` N+1 fix may require a Protocol change upstream if `_fetch_issues_graphql` does not return sub-issue counts. Medium effort because of the API contract surface, not because of algorithmic complexity.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer relying on this plugin**, I want to **githubtaskprovider makes redundant plan-existence guard api call before every task operation** so that **the tool works correctly and reliably**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Simplify review of P912 \u2014 .tmp/scratch/reports/simplify-efficiency-P912.md\\n- **Priority**: P2\\n- **Added**: 2026-04-05\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n\\n---\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"0ea7fee2ee07b4d8d4968049c3e27c8ff235f5a446eddc4ca64240144f180a16\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABSdr4wQ\"}"}